From f0eae04ebea6c3d322aec9dd16a549c9af900f7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:33:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Screenshot] An HDR screenshot is saved with an SDR copy next
 to it

A screenshot taken while the output is HDR is the picture as the display
gets it, PQ (or HLG) with BT.2020 primaries, tagged so in a cICP chunk.
Only a viewer that reads that tag shows it right; every other one shows
it dark and pale (issue #30), and a copy that loses the tag on the way
looks so everywhere.

Next to it, screenshotNNNNN-sdr.png (the name of the screenshot with
"-sdr" before its extension) is now written as well: the same capture
tone mapped to SDR by swscale (perceptual intent), with BT.709 primaries
and the sRGB transfer, tagged sRGB (an sRGB chunk, no cICP), as Kodi's
SDR screenshots are, so that any viewer shows it right. The HDR file is
written first, by the same call with the same pixels and tags as
before; the copy is not written where it fails. Both are written on the
worker of the capture service, never on the thread that draws; a 4K
copy takes about half a second of one core on the build host.

The peak it is mapped from (CaptureSourcePeak()) is the MaxCLL of the
content where it is given (100 cd/m2 or more) and lower than the peak of
its mastering display, else that peak; where neither is known, 1000
cd/m2, the most usual mastering peak, instead of the 10000 swscale takes,
which darkens the whole picture. swscale reads only the mastering
display, so that peak is given to it there. The bookmark thumbnails
(CaptureToBGRA()) are mapped from the same peak.

kodi.log says, at INFO, which copy was written and from which peak.

Tests: TestCaptureConvert, the peak of a capture, the copy of a PQ ramp
(black 0, 203 cd/m2 169, the peak 255), its sRGB coding against the
BT.709 of the thumbnails, no metadata as 1000, MaxCLL taken, no copy of
SDR, the copy of HLG.
